re practical approach — I'll write a script to capture the raw observation, apply the flip, and then inspect the image characteristics to see which orientation actually looks correct.\n\nActually, I realize the real clue is in the behavior: when flip is OFF, the robot approaches the soup correctly, but when it's ON, the robot moves away. So flip OFF is giving the model the right image. The actual problem is why the grasp itself is failing — at the moment of grasping, the end effector and gripper positions show the robot isn't quite aligned with the soup target.\n\nLooking at the coordinates, the end effector is about 4 centimeters off in the x-axis and overshooting in the y-axis, which means the gripper is probably closing on empty space or just the edge of the can. This positioning error could stem from the image transformation being incomplete — maybe I only need a vertical flip instead of flipping both axes, or there's an issue with how the center crop is being applied.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1433,"cache_read_input_tokens":83788,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1433},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"20edf378-2c29-4db1-9f30-77d38d22d300","uuid":"a9293188-d4e7-4e12-9d37-e08b0ba8cde3"}
